              "isInner": false,
              "settings": {
                "html": "<script type=\"application/ld+json\">{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\", \"@type\": \"FAQPage\", \"mainEntity\": [{\"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"Is VAT on restaurant food 20%?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": {\"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"Food eaten on your premises is standard-rated at 20%. Hot takeaway food is also 20%. Most cold takeaway food, such as a sandwich to go, is zero-rated. Alcohol and soft drinks are 20% whether eaten in or taken away.\"}}, {\"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"What is a good GP% for a restaurant?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": {\"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"Many UK restaurants aim for a food gross profit of around 65\\u201370% and a drinks gross profit of 70% or more. The right target depends on your menu, pricing and concept.\"}}, {\"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"What is prime cost?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": {\"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"Prime cost is your food and drink costs plus your labour costs. Many hospitality businesses aim to keep it under 60\\u201365% of sales, so there is enough left for rent, bills and profit.\"}}, {\"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"How much does it cost to employ someone in the UK in 2026/27?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": {\"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"On top of gross pay, you pay employer National Insurance at 15% on earnings over \\u00a35,000 a year, plus at least 3% employer pension on qualifying earnings. Many small employers can claim Employment Allowance of up to \\u00a310,500 a year against their employer NI.\"}}, {\"@type\": \"Question\", \"name\": \"Are these calculators tax advice?\", \"acceptedAnswer\": {\"@type\": \"Answer\", \"text\": \"No. They give estimates for guidance. Speak to an F&A hospitality accountant for advice on your own figures.\"}}]}</script>",
